  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nWednesday night through Friday\nAssessment...very high (>90%) confidence of widespread heavy rain\n\nRound 2 of heavy rain will occur Wednesday night into Thursday \nmorning. The probabilities of heavy rain are the highest during this \ntime frame as PWATS are just over 2 inches and the overall forcing \nwill be enhanced with the passage of a weak surface low.\n\nThe axis of the heaviest rain looks to run from Cedar Rapids up to \nDubuque; thus there will be some overlap between round 1 and 2. Like \nthe previous night the probability of rainfall rates exceeding 0.25 \ninches/hour is 30-40 percent. Thus this will be another long period \nof light to moderate rainfall with periods of heavy rain. \nThunderstorms will be possible but are still expected to remain \nisolated to low end scattered (20-30%).\n\nAnother period of spotty showers and drizzle looks to occur Thursday \nafternoon before round 3 begins late Thursday afternoon into \nThursday night.\n\nUnlike the previous two rounds of heavy rain, this round will be \nfurther to the south and east. Right now the rough axis looks to be \nfrom roughly Keokuk, IA to Peru, IL. Given the southward trend with \nthe heavy rain, the possibility does exist that the axis of round 3 \ncould be further to the south and east. Thunderstorms will be \npossible but will remain isolated to low end scattered (20-30%).\n\nLike the previous two round of heavy rain, the probability of \nrainfall rates exceeding 0.25 inches/hour are 30-40%. \n\nBy late Thursday night and into Friday morning, rain will end from \nnorthwest to southeast. Friday afternoon looks to be cool with \ntemperatures only in the 60s.\n\nFriday night through Tuesday\nAssessment...medium to high confidence (50-70%) of dry and cool\nconditions\n\nStarting this weekend and continuing into the first half of next \nweek cool fall-like temperatures are expected.  The global models \nhave been advertising this scenario for over a week but have slowly \nshifted the brunt of the coolest air to the eastern Great Lakes and \nNew England.",
